{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 46, :comments ["<b>All the days of its quarantine.</b> And not the days when the mark has peeled off. One might think I exclude the definitively afflicted one whose mark has peeled off — therefore Scripture says ״all the days.״" "<b>He is impure until evening.</b> This teaches that he does not defile garments. One might think even if he lingered there the time it takes to eat a פְּרָס — therefore Scripture says ״and the one who eats in the house shall wash his garments.״ I have only one who eats; one who reclines — from where? Scripture says ״and the one who reclines.״ I have only one who eats and one who reclines; neither eating nor reclining — from where? Scripture says <b>shall wash, shall wash</b> — it multiplies. If so, why are ״eats״ and ״reclines״ stated? To set the measure for the one who reclines as the time it takes to eat a פְּרָס (ibid.)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:06:31.286750065Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}